<title>INDIA: SBI Capital's Indian regional cement projections.</title>
<headline>SBI Capital's Indian regional cement projections.</headline>
<dateline>BOMBAY 1996-11-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Securities firm SBI Capital Markets has projected cement supply/demand equation in Indian cement markets until the year 2000.</p>
<p>&quot;Although the all-India scenario presents a near balanced position, we expect pockets of surplus and deficit to develop across regions,&quot; SBI Capital said in a cement sector review.</p>
<p>&quot;The worst affected will be the northern region with a 13 percent over supply by 2000. While the eastern and parts of the western region will witness oversupply, the southern region will continue to be cement deficit,&quot; it said.</p>
<p>Overall, cement supply will grow at a compounded annual growth rate of 11 percent, resulting in a manageable surplus of about 4.0 million tonnes per annum in 1999/2000, it said.</p>
<p>&quot;We believe regional players with locational advantage in terms of proximity to markets and ability to overcome infrastructure bottlenecks will have the competitive edge.&quot;</p>
